WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.440
So, you want to bring back the Woolly Mammoth.&nbsp;
“Bring back Woolly Mammoths” Maybe you love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.320
elephants, maybe you love science, or maybe you&nbsp;
just want to make a lot of money. Either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.480
we are remarkably close to being able to&nbsp;
do it. And the opportunity here is a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.040
bigger than most people think. Naturally,&nbsp;
there are tons of skeptics: “Bad idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.200
Did no one see Jurassic Park?” And sure, no one&nbsp;
wants a group of massive Woolly Mammoth’s charging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.520
around killing people. But this project could&nbsp;
have a profoundly positive impact on the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.720
so let’s dig into it and figure out how it&nbsp;
would work, what the benefits are, and lastly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:37.040
how much money it could actually make. Genetic&nbsp;
engineering is a complex issue and I don’t think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.720
we should ignore the ethical questions: “Yeah,&nbsp;
your scientists were so preoccupied with whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.240
or not they could, they didn’t stop to think&nbsp;
if they should.” So let’s take Jeff Goldblum’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:48.640
advice and stop to think about whether or not&nbsp;
we should actually resurrect the Woolly Mammoth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.120
First up is the why: these creatures have been&nbsp;
dead for thousands of years. Do we really need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:02.320
them? And could they even survive if we did&nbsp;
bring them back? Well unlike the Dinosaurs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.680
which died-off millions of years ago. Mammoths&nbsp;
and humans co-existed for millennia, at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:13.040
until we killed them all. “Early man ate the&nbsp;
woolly mammoth out of existence 12,000 years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:17.840
Most of them went extinct because we ate them.”&nbsp;
So you could argue that humanity has some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.640
moral obligation to bring them back, since we were&nbsp;
the ones responsible for their ultimate demise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.520
But there’s a bigger reason for bringing them&nbsp;
back. And it’s a lot more selfish: bringing back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.400
the Woolly Mammoth could actually help prevent&nbsp;
climate change, and that benefits us directly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.920
See, the permafrost is melting and that’s a big&nbsp;
problem for us. We need to keep these icy regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.320
frozen as long as possible, if we want to control&nbsp;
carbon emissions: “And the reason we want lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.600
temperatures in the Tundra is that there are 14&nbsp;
hundred billion tons of carbon locked up, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.520
too far from the surface, in the icy soil and it’s&nbsp;
melting very quickly.” Woolly Mammoths accomplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.880
several really important tasks up in the Tundra.&nbsp;
They knock down trees and clear whole forests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.520
This is a bit counterintuitive, because we think&nbsp;
of trees as generally important to environmental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:08.880
protection, but up in the Arctic, clearer&nbsp;
grasslands actually reflect more sunlight and stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:14.160
colder. As Mammoths walk around the Tundra, they&nbsp;
pack down the snow and create sheets of ice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.040
actually keep the permafrost colder. There are&nbsp;
bison up in the tundra, and they do a pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.160
job of clearing smaller shrubs, but they can’t&nbsp;
knock down whole trees. So repopulating northern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:28.800
Russia and Canada with Woolly Mammoths might&nbsp;
actually be able to slow the permafrost melt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.200
buy us some extra time to fight climate change.&nbsp;
It isn’t going to solve everything, but it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.920
like it’s worth a shot. But there are other great&nbsp;
reasons to support this project. It’s just plain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.840
inspiring. I firmly believe that seeing astronauts&nbsp;
go to space is incredibly inspirational. Any child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.920
that sees a rocket launch immediately wants to&nbsp;
learn more about the science and technology that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.120
made it possible. That’s really important, since&nbsp;
physics isn’t exactly the easiest subject. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.760
Woolly Mammoth project could do the same for the&nbsp;
biological sciences. Seeing this ancient creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.800
reincarnated would undoubtedly inspire a new&nbsp;
generation of biologists and they would go on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.992
do all sorts of great things. Some would work on&nbsp;
curing cancer, others would help fight infectious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.992 --> 00:03:05.200
diseases, but all of these new scientists would&nbsp;
help push humanity forward. We shouldn’t discount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.120
the application of de-extinction technology&nbsp;
more broadly though. The Woolly Mammoth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.280
fun to talk about because it’s such a striking&nbsp;
creature. Everyone knows what a Woolly Mammoth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.840
is and who doesn’t want to see one in real life?&nbsp;
But the same genetic technology that will help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.200
create a new Mammoth will also help restore other&nbsp;
species on the brink of extinction. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:27.680
actually already happening. The black footed&nbsp;
ferret is one great example. This species was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.720
declared extinct in 1980, but just last year,&nbsp;
scientists were able to clone a black footed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:37.760
ferret that had died decades earlier to help bring&nbsp;
the species back. And this happens all the time:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.240
a particular species becomes so rare that there&nbsp;
isn’t enough genetic diversity in the remaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.360
animals to continue reproducing. But cloning older&nbsp;
animals can help prevent outright extinction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:51.520
Clearly there are risks to any cutting edge&nbsp;
science, but we aren’t talking about resurrecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.880
deadly dinosaurs after all, these Woolly Mammoths&nbsp;
are remarkably close to elephants. And elephants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.600
have been outmatched by modern firearms for&nbsp;
decades. But you’re probably asking yourself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.240
how would this even work? “Can you bring back&nbsp;
the Woolly Mammoth? Because I would love to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:10.640
one. I would love a Woolly steak.” Well, if you’ve&nbsp;
seen Jurassic Park, you’ll probably remember that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:15.840
the original dinosaur DNA was found preserved in&nbsp;
amber, and something remarkably similar happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.280
with the Woolly Mammoth. It’s basically impossible&nbsp;
for us to find intact dinosaur DNA due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.800
natural decay that occurs over millions of years.&nbsp;
Even if blood was preserved in amber, radiation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.920
would have ripped the DNA sequences to shreds over&nbsp;
such a long period of time. But the Mammoths only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:37.040
died off thousands, not millions of years ago.&nbsp;
And many Mammoths were frozen in ice as soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:41.920
they died, which preserved them remarkably well.&nbsp;
And the story of how we found these incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:47.760
specimens is actually pretty crazy too. Ivory is&nbsp;
one of the most valuable materials in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.720
and the only way to get it is from elephants, or&nbsp;
at least it used to be. With elephant populations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.760
dwindling, governments started restricting the&nbsp;
sale of elephant ivory. But Russian ivory hunters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:04.640
figured out an alternative. “After embargo,&nbsp;
for elephant, people start to sell Mammoth.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.120
There were no restrictions on digging up frozen&nbsp;
Mammoths and selling the ivory from their tusks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.680
so a small industry started to flourish. “Life,&nbsp;
uh, finds a way” And as these Mammoth hunters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.880
dug deeper and deeper they started finding bodies&nbsp;
that were so perfectly frozen, they appeared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.600
actually bleed when they were cut open. This was&nbsp;
an incredibly promising breakthrough, since these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.640
preserved specimens might have enough DNA left&nbsp;
in them to piece together a complete sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:36.160
“This is the stuff that could possibly clone and&nbsp;
bring back the mammoth from its extinction. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:42.000
in other words, I’m literally holding a plot line&nbsp;
from Jurassic Park in my hands.” Now, to be clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.120
we haven’t found any living mammoth cells,&nbsp;
just DNA fragments. And that’s an important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:52.000
distinction. Scientists essentially need to piece&nbsp;
together these fragments to understand what genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.400
produce the traits that allowed the Woolly Mammoth&nbsp;
to thrive in such harsh environments. It would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:00.960
great if we could just relocate some African or&nbsp;
Asian elephants to the tundra and call it a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.640
but they wouldn’t last long up there.&nbsp;
It’s just too cold. The Woolly Mammoth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.280
genetically similar to today’s elephants, but&nbsp;
has a few key traits that allow it to survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:14.320
in low temperatures. Thick fur, extra layers&nbsp;
of fat, and hemoglobin to carry oxygen in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:19.360
blood are all critical differentiators, but&nbsp;
the DNA sequences that code for these genes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.360
are pretty well understood now. “Right&nbsp;
now, a team of geneticists at Harvard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:29.760
run by a narcoleptic, dyslexic genius, named Dr.&nbsp;
George Church, are on the verge of bringing back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.520
the Woolly Mammoth.” George Church is at the&nbsp;
center of this project and just co-founded a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.400
company named Colossal to commercialize the effort&nbsp;
to clone a Woolly Mammoth. And he’s not just some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.000
crazy scientist. Well, maybe he is a bit crazy,&nbsp;
but he is definitely well respected within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.440
scientific community. In 1984, Church published&nbsp;
the first direct genome sequencing method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.440
and played a critical role in the human genome&nbsp;
project. He’s been working on this Mammoth project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:57.600
for the last 6 years, and he clearly really&nbsp;
believes in it: “This is real, not science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:03.920
fiction, and potentially applicable to endangered&nbsp;
and extinct species.” There are two major trends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:08.560
that are simultaneously accelerating to make&nbsp;
something like this a real possibility. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:14.320
the cost of DNA sequencing is falling incredibly&nbsp;
fast. Twenty years ago, sequencing a single genome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:20.080
cost $100 million dollars, now, it costs less&nbsp;
than $1 thousand dollars. Biology is becoming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:25.200
drastically more accessible, and that’s opening&nbsp;
up tons of new opportunities. The second major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:30.640
trend is in gene editing technology. CRISPR is the&nbsp;
latest development in this area, and the creators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:35.360
just won the Nobel Prize for their work on it last&nbsp;
year. You can think of these two developments like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.800
reading and writing. In order to clone a Mammoth,&nbsp;
we need to be able to read old Mammoth DNA and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:46.720
then write that DNA into a new embryo. The cost&nbsp;
of reading DNA has fallen precipitously, and now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:52.640
the cost of writing DNA is also falling. But even&nbsp;
with these advances, directly cloning a Mammoth is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:58.240
still a bit out of reach. Luckily, scientists have&nbsp;
found a workaround. Instead of trying to create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:03.120
perfect replica, they are just going to focus&nbsp;
on adding those key genes I mentioned earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:07.680
into an Asian elephant, essentially creating a&nbsp;
hybrid. “Now, this wouldn’t be an exact replica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:11.520
because the short DNA fragments that I told you&nbsp;
about will prevent us from building the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.840
structure, but it would make something that looked&nbsp;
and felt very much like a woolly mammoth did." And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:21.040
this is a really key strategy. The team working on&nbsp;
this project isn’t getting hung up on satisfying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.960
some vague technical notion of what constitutes&nbsp;
a Mammoth. They just want to make something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:30.720
will get the job done in the Tundra. But now, to&nbsp;
the really vital part: the economic opportunity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:35.760
Church spun his research out into a full company,&nbsp;
and they just raised $15 million dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:39.840
so they have to start thinking about turning a&nbsp;
profit. The first and most obvious way for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:44.640
company, Colossal, to make money is just to sell&nbsp;
cloned Mammoths. This is pretty straightforward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.640
Countries that have melting permafrost could&nbsp;
buy Mammoths from Colossal directly and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:52.880
turn them loose in areas that are most affected&nbsp;
by warming climates. But there could be plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:57.840
of other buyers. Zoos already spend a fortune on&nbsp;
rare animals and a cloned Woolly Mammoth would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.720
clearly be the rarest of all. Pandas routinely&nbsp;
sell for over $1 million dollars, and they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:08.240
only endangered. There’s also a long history of&nbsp;
cloned animals selling for incredibly high prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:13.920
Sir Lancelot Encore is a cloned dog estimated to&nbsp;
be worth millions of dollars, and a company named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:19.120
Sinogene clones cats and dogs regularly for around&nbsp;
$50 thousand dollars a piece. Missy the Cow sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:24.560
for $1.2 million dollars in 2009, and she wasn’t&nbsp;
even cloned. So there’s clearly a market for rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:28.720
animals with very specific DNA sequences. I&nbsp;
could definitely see the market for cloned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.920
Mammoths being extremely lucrative, even if it was&nbsp;
only for a few years before the novelty wore off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:38.560
After all, one of the investors in Colossal's&nbsp;
funding round was the TikTok star Josh Richards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:42.320
He hasn’t disclosed how much money he put in,&nbsp;
but I wouldn’t be surprised if he could make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:46.960
it all back, just by being the first influencer&nbsp;
to do a dance on top of a Woolly Mammoth. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:51.200
there are other ways that this company could make&nbsp;
money, and they might not even need to sell any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.480
Mammoths to do it. Church teamed up with a serial&nbsp;
entrepreneur named Ben Lamm to found Colossal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:01.440
and Ben has an interesting history in startups.&nbsp;
His last company was an artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:05.280
business named Hypergiant. If you’ve never&nbsp;
heard of them, that’s to be expected. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.000
though they have a really slick website, they&nbsp;
don’t make consumer-facing products. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.480
the company focuses on partnerships with big&nbsp;
companies who operate in the aerospace and defense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.200
industry. So this project could be an attempt&nbsp;
to do something similar. Attract a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:24.080
really incredible talent to an ambitious project&nbsp;
and then make money licensing out intellectual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:28.640
property and contracting with other biotech&nbsp;
companies. Either way, Church clearly takes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:34.400
implications of his work very seriously: “We are&nbsp;
taking more and more control over our biological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:40.320
destiny” The ability to both read and write&nbsp;
DNA is a truly incredible breakthrough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:44.480
and one that we shouldn’t take lightly. We need&nbsp;
to be careful about the second order effects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:49.600
these projects, but that shouldn’t slow us down.&nbsp;
Big problems require big ambitious solutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.760
and I, for one, hope this project&nbsp;
succeeds. Thanks for watching.

